# LiNC ladder PLC service - example HAL wiring
#
# linc-plc is a userspace PLC: it loads a ladder project JSON, compiles it with
# linc-plc-core, creates its own linc_plc.* HAL component and scans the ladder
# in its own process. It never touches the linc.* HMI component; those five
# outputs stay the closed LiNC surface they are.
#
# THIS FILE IS DOCUMENTATION ONLY.
#
#   * It is not loaded by default. No machine INI or HAL file in this tree
#     includes it; copy the lines you need into the machine's own HAL file.
#   * It is not a safety function. linc-plc drives ordinary HAL outputs with
#     no safety rating, no redundancy and no independent watchdog. E-stop,
#     limits, guards and interlocks stay in the machine HAL and in the
#     physical circuit.
#   * The pin names below follow the example ladder project, not a fixed
#     contract: there is one linc_plc.<binding> pin per I/O binding in the
#     project, so edit the nets to match your project. The pin map is captured
#     when the daemon starts; changing the project's I/O bindings needs a
#     daemon restart (the socket answers Load with "io_map_locked").
#
# Pins linc-plc creates, in this order, before it calls hal_ready():
#
#   linc_plc.<binding>     HAL_BIT  in   for every ladder "In" binding
#   linc_plc.<binding>     HAL_BIT  out  for every ladder "Out" binding
#   linc_plc.alive         HAL_BIT  out  toggles every scan while the ladder runs,
#                                       held false in Idle, Loaded and Fault
#   linc_plc.scan-count    HAL_S32  out  wrapping scan counter
#   linc_plc.scan-time-us  HAL_S32  out  duration of the last scan, microseconds
#   linc_plc.fault         HAL_BIT  out  set when the scan loop faulted or stopped
#   linc_plc.overruns      HAL_S32  out  wrapping count of scans that ran long
#
# Example ladder project bindings used below: inputs estop, oil-lube, air-ok;
# outputs worklamp, chip-conveyor, cycle-ready.

# --- example, do not load blindly -------------------------------------------
#
# loadusr -W linc-plc --project /home/operator/linc/ladder.json
#
# # Ladder inputs read from the machine's own rio.* signals. rio.estop-in.bit
# # and rio.machine-on-switch.bit are rio outputs (HAL sources); each signal
# # below drives exactly one linc_plc input pin.
# net ladder-estop rio.estop-in.bit => linc_plc.estop
# net ladder-on    rio.machine-on-switch.bit => linc_plc.air-ok
#
# # Ladder outputs consumed by the machine. rio.worklamp.bit and
# # rio.tool-change.bit are rio inputs (HAL sinks); one writer per signal.
# net ladder-worklamp linc_plc.worklamp => rio.worklamp.bit
# net ladder-chip     linc_plc.chip-conveyor => rio.oil-lube.bit
#
# # The heartbeat: linc_plc.alive changes state every scan while the ladder is
# # running, so a consumer that stops seeing edges knows the scan loop stalled,
# # the ladder is not running, or the daemon exited. It is held false in Idle,
# # Loaded and Fault and on the way out, so the watchdog condition is
# #
# #     alive must show edges  AND  linc_plc.fault must be false
# #
# # Requiring only the edges is not enough, and requiring only "not fault" is
# # not enough either. Net both into the watchdog; a bare net only makes the
# # pins visible in halcmd/halscope.
# net plc-alive linc_plc.alive
#
# # Fault. The daemon raises this after a HAL error and on the way out; wire it
# # to the lamp or input the machine already uses for a controller fault.
# net plc-fault linc_plc.fault => cnc_panel.led_plc
#
# # Optional: scan timing on the diagnostics scope.
# net plc-scan-time linc_plc.scan-time-us
#
# Failure behaviour you are wiring against: on Stop, on a HAL error and on
# daemon exit, linc-plc drives every ladder output false and sets
# linc_plc.fault. A clean stop then removes the component (hal_exit). A panic
# or a release build's abort leaves the last values latched in HAL, which is
# why the machine needs the alive heartbeat on a real watchdog rather than a
# plain net.
